7b450e43ef19364aff129b7147df7ea3af41b94b18e49f9e51f.jpg\",\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/video/a34ef56f9a2c45756f1ae45eed227690708aeaba71bd859d087e1caf7be5e910.mp4\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"1324628590732912\",\"name\":\"Dr. Eleanor Carter\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/profile_picture/1324628590732912.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"1324628590732912\",\"liveAdsCount\":0,\"reach30d\":63644,\"totalReach\":879975,\"countriesCount\":4,\"facebookLikes\":1808,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"Looking to Support Healthy Blood Sugar Naturally? 🌿\\n\\nExperience the Power of Metabolae Sri Lankan Ceylon Cinnamon!\\n\\n✅ Helps support healthy blood sugar levels\\n✅ Supports steady daily energy\\n✅ Helps reduce blood sugar crashes\\n✅ Authentic Sri Lankan Ceylon cinnamon\\n✅ 200+ Clinical studies\\n✅ Enhanced with MCT oil for absorption\\n\\nSupport healthier glucose metabolism naturally with research-backed ingredients designed for daily stability.\",\"transcript\":\"Is it true you can flush all the sugar out of your blood through your urine? Yes. And it's something ninety eight percent of diabetics have no clue about. I'm a pancreatic cancer research doctor, and if I were gonna die tomorrow, I'd want every diabetic, including you, to stop asking God to deliver you from type two diabetes. He already sent the answer. You're the one refusing to reach for it. Now I'm gonna spit in the face of big pharma and expose the three biggest lies they tell you about type two diabetes. By the end, you'll know how to address the real problem once and for all. Give me sixty seconds to explain. Lie number one. Type two diabetes is caused by eating too much sugar or getting old. That's outdated. Type two diabetes is actually a cellular door disease. The doors on your cells stopped opening. Insulin knocks, but nothing answers. So every gram of sugar from every meal stays trapped in your bloodstream with nowhere to go. That trapped sugar grinds through the lining of every blood vessel in your body, after day, year after year, destroying your feet, your kidneys, your eyes, and your heart. That's why cutting carbs, fasting, or walking for hours does almost nothing. The doors are still closed. The sugar is still trapped. The grinding never stops. Lie number two, metformin or Ozempic fixes the problem. That's ridiculous. Metformin tells your liver to make less sugar. It never opens the doors. Ozempic slows your stomach, so less sugar enters your blood. It never opens the doors. Yardiance forces sugar out through your kidneys and wears them down while it does it. None of them touch the trapped sugar that's already grinding through your vessels right now. It's like turning down the faucet while the drain is clogged. The water stays. Lie number three. Type two diabetes is irreversible, and you'll be managing it until the day you die. It's criminal that they keep telling you this. Research has identified two compounds from true Ceylon cinnamon that address both halves. The first is called MHCP. It signals the doors on your cells to start opening again. And once those doors open, the trapped sugar finally moves out of your blood and into your cells where it gets burned for energy, the grinding stops. The second is called cinnamaldehyde. It repairs the vessel lining the trapped sugar has been destroying for years. It helps your vessels produce nitric oxide again, the signal that tells them to relax and carry blood back to the places it stopped reaching. Your feet, your kidneys, your eyes, your heart, two compounds, two jobs from one bark. And the best part, you take one soft gel every morning with breakfast, and that's it. Thousands of women are already using this to finally feel their feet again. They wake up with energy they haven't had in years. They sleep through the night. They check their fasting glucose and stare at the number because they don't believe it. Their doctors ask what changed, stunned, because their lab panels look different for the first time in a decade. But as you can imagine, big pharma making billions off diabetics like you is furious about it right now. They'd rather keep you on monthly prescriptions for the rest of your life than let you take one soft gel that costs less than a dollar a day. That's why I'm leaving the link below to Metabolay, the only product that gets all three variables right. DNA verified true Ceylon cinnamon from Sri Lanka. Not cassia from China, like 90% of what's on shelves. Concentrated 12 to one to match the clinical research dose, seven thousand two hundred milligram equivalent per soft gel. And suspended in MCT oil so the fat soluble compounds actually cross your gut wall and reach your bloodstream instead of passing straight through you like every dry capsule on Amazon. It comes with a full ninety day money back guarantee. So you can start doing this at home today. No prescription your doctor has ever written offered you a refund. Every batch is third party tested before it reaches you, which is why it works while the cheap cinnamon on Amazon does nothing for your blood sugar and quietly poisons your liver with coumarin while it does nothing. Tap the link below right now. And if the sale is live, consider yourself blessed. They make small batches. When it sells out, it takes time to source. True Ceylon from Sri Lanka at this concentration cannot be rushed. Two compounds, two jobs, one soft gel. That's the answer. Don't waste this chance.\",\"callToAction\":\"LEARN MORE\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://metabolae.com/products/softgels-ceylon-standard\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"metabolae.com\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Ceylon Cinnamon 7200mg Equivalent with MCT Oil\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":null},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":646615,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":5820,\"duplicates\":7,\"reachDelta1d\":null,\"reachDelta7d\":null,\"reachDelta30d\":16328},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":[\"AU\",\"CA\",\"GB\",\"US\"],\"mainCountry\":\"GB\",\"gender\":null,\"ageMin\":null,\"ageMax\":null},\"rank\":{\"positionInPage\":1,\"currentRank\":1,\"rankDelta\":null,\"improvementPct\":0.26},\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":null,\"isLowReach\":false,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}},{\"id\":\"facebook_1024052563854382\",\"collationId\":\"28216118474660792\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"active\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-07-28T19:27:38.384Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-07-27T07:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-08-01T20:40:51.428Z\",\"daysRunning\":123,\"media\":{\"type\":\"image\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/image/20c42fabb0c4fa5ed96e15331c1648807f43590c41e8eaf0b19e26dce53e9fa5.png\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"1018883361310799\",\"name\":\"Sandra Keller, RN\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/profile_picture/1018883361310799.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"1018883361310799\",\"liveAdsCount\":585,\"reach30d\":657004,\"totalReach\":4231542,\"countriesCount\":5,\"facebookLikes\":40683,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"I'm a nurse and I've been administering M*tformin for 28 years, but last month I refused to take it myself.\\n\\nI want to explain why. Because there's a sentence I've been legally unable to say in my professional capacity for close to three decades. And until three months ago, I kept my mouth shut.\\n\\nI'm not keeping it shut anymore.\\n\\nMy name is Sandra. I work in the dialysis unit at a hospital I'm not going to name. 28 years in the same unit. I've sat with more diabetic patients than I can count. Most of them had perfectly controlled A1Cs on the charts they carried in with them.\\n\\nThat's the part that broke me eventually. I just didn't know it yet.\\n\\nHere's what my job looks like. A patient wheels in for their scheduled treatment. Three days a week, four hours a chair. I pull up their chart, past and present. A1C 6.8. Fasting glucose 128. They've been on M*tformin for eight, ten, twelve years before they landed on my unit. The numbers looked textbook the whole way in.\\n\\nAnd now they're on my dialysis chair because their kidneys quit.\\n\\nAnd they start telling me what else is going on.\\n\\nThe feet that burn at 3am.\\n\\nThe vision that blurs in the afternoon.\\n\\nThe bathroom trips they used to make with foam in the toilet, back before there was nothing left to filter.\\n\\nThe thing they don't want to say out loud about the bedroom.\\n\\nThe chest pressure on the transfer from the car to the chair they keep telling themselves is nothing.\\n\\nAnd every chart in front of me said they were controlled.\\n\\nThe first few times you see it you tell yourself it's age. Or genetics. Or they cheated on their diet. By year five you stop telling yourself stories. By year ten you start counting.\\n\\nI started counting in 2008. I keep a number in my head now. I'm not going to share it because it would sound like I'm making it up. Just trust me when I say it's a lot of people. A lot of grandparents. A lot of fathers. A lot of men and women in their fifties, sixties, seventies, all who thought they were doing everything right.\\n\\nAll of them on M*tformin. All of them with controlled A1Cs. All of them lost the kidneys before they got to me. And now they're losing the feet, the eyes, the bedroom, the energy, one quiet inch at a time on the chair.\\n\\nAnd a smaller group of them I see disappear from the schedule. Because the heart attack their controlled A1C never warned them was coming.\\n\\nAnd I never said anything.\\n\\nI want to be honest about that. Because you might be reading this thinking I'm some kind of whistleblower hero. I'm not. I'm a nurse who took an oath, who has a license to protect, who has a mortgage to pay, and who has been told for 28 years that if I question a doctor's prescription out loud in front of a patient I will lose all three.\\n\\nThat's not a metaphor. That's a real rule.\\n\\nWhen a doctor writes a script for M*tformin, my job is to hand it to the patient and explain how to take it. If the patient asks me do you think this will help, I'm allowed to say your doctor knows best. I'm not allowed to say what I actually think. I'm not allowed to mention what I've seen. I'm not allowed to tell them about the man in chair four whose chart looked exactly like theirs eight years ago before he ended up on my unit.\\n\\nIf I do, I'm out of a job. Possibly out of a career.\\n\\nSo I learned to nod. To smile. To explain the dosing schedule. To go home and try to forget. And then I'd come back the next shift and do it again.\\n\\nThat was the deal I made for 28 years.\\n\\nThree months ago the deal blew up.\\n\\nI had my annual physical. Routine bloodwork. I'm 59, I exercise four days a week, I cook most of my own food, I haven't been overweight a day in my adult life. I wasn't worried about the labs.\\n\\nThe results came back and my A1C was 6.9. Fasting glucose 144.\\n\\nI read the number twice. Then I read it again. Then I sat in my car in the parking lot for almost an hour because I knew exactly what was coming next.\\n\\nTwo days later my primary care doctor called me. Sandra, your numbers are concerning. I'd like to start you on M*tformin 500. We can talk about it at your follow-up but I'm sending the prescription to your pharmacy today.\\n\\nHe didn't ask. He told.\\n\\n28 years of nodding and smiling collapsed in about four seconds. I drove home from work that night and I sat at my kitchen table and I cried. Not because I was scared of diabetes. Because I knew I was about to walk into the same machine I'd been watching swallow patients for almost three decades.\\n\\nAnd that machine doesn't fix anything. I have spent 28 years watching it not fix anything. I have spent 28 years watching the endpoint of it. Chair four. Chair seven. Chair twelve.\\n\\nI let the prescription sit at the pharmacy. I didn't pick it up. I told my husband I needed a week to think about it. He's a paramedic. He's seen the same things I've seen from the other side. He didn't argue.\\n\\nThat week is when everything changed for me.\\n\\nI started doing what I'd never let myself do as a working nurse. I started actually reading. Not the drug company pamphlets. Not the clinical guidelines. The original studies. The stuff buried in the back of medical journals. The research nobody hands you on the floor because it complicates the story you're being paid to tell.\\n\\nAnd I learned something I now cannot believe I didn't know.\\n\\nThe number on your A1C was never the thing that was going to hurt you.\\n\\nI want to say that again because it took me a week to actually accept it. The number on your A1C was never the thing that was going to hurt you. It was never the thing hurting my patients either.\\n\\nHere's what's actually happening.\\n\\nYour body uses sugar for energy. Every time you eat, sugar moves into your blood. From there it's supposed to go into your cells. Your muscle cells. Your liver cells. The places that need fuel. There are tiny doors on every cell, and when insulin tells those doors to open, the sugar moves out of your blood and into the cells where it gets burned.\\n\\nThat's how a healthy body works.\\n\\nBut after years of constant carb intake, the doors stop responding. They stop opening when insulin knocks. So the sugar can't get in. It stays in your blood instead.\\n\\nAnd here's the part nobody told me in nursing school the way it needed to be said.\\n\\nThat trapped sugar doesn't just sit in your blood waiting. It moves. Every heartbeat. Every meal. Every day. It's traveling through every vessel in your body. And as it travels it grinds against the inside of the vessels themselves. The endothelium. The thin layer of cells that lines every artery, vein, and capillary. The sugar coats the proteins there, damages them, and over years the lining stiffens and stops working the way it's supposed to.\\n\\nNow look at what that does.\\n\\nThe vessels feeding the nerves in your feet. They stop working. That's the burning at 3am. The tingling. The numbness that creeps up the leg. The amputation conversation that comes eight years later.\\n\\nThe vessels feeding the filters in your kidneys. They stop working. That's the protein leaking into your urine. The foam in the toilet most patients never mention. The creatinine creeping up on the panel. The dialysis machine I'm hooking them up to.\\n\\nThe vessels in the back of your eye. They stop working. That's the blurry vision. The floaters. The diabetic retinopathy a patient first hears about when they're already losing it.\\n\\nThe vessels feeding the erectile tissue. They stop working. That's the ED no man tells me about until I've been his nurse for three months of treatments. The intimate life that ends without a conversation.\\n\\nAnd eventually the small arteries feeding the heart muscle. That's the heart attack. The one diabetic men have at multiples the rate of every other man. Often before they even know their A1C has been quietly climbing.\\n\\nIt is not five different complications.\\n\\nIt is the same trapped sugar attacking the same kind of tissue in five different parts of the body.\\n\\nOne disease. Five faces. Every one of them traceable to the sugar that can't get out of the blood.\\n\\nAnd here is the part that broke my brain when I finally sat with it.\\n\\nYour body knows the sugar is stuck. So your pancreas keeps making more insulin, trying to force the doors open. For years. Until eventually the pancreas can't keep up. And the number on your A1C starts climbing, even though the damage has been happening the whole time.\\n\\nThat's why your number won't come down.\\n\\nNow I'm going to tell you what M*tformin actually does, because I had to sit with this myself before I could believe what I was reading.\\n\\nM*tformin works on one specific thing. It tells your liver to make less sugar. That's the whole mechanism. It reduces how much sugar your liver is releasing into your blood. The number on your chart drops because there's less sugar entering circulation. Your doctor says great. Insurance is happy. The chart looks beautiful.\\n\\nBut the sugar that's already in your blood? Still trapped. Still moving with every heartbeat. Still grinding through your vessels. The drug never touches the doors. It just reduces the supply.\\n\\nLowering the supply doesn't unlock the doors. It just hides how stuck they are.\\n\\nThat's why my patients kept ending up in front of me ten years later with controlled A1Cs and kidneys that couldn't filter and numb feet and dead bedrooms and heart attacks that arrived without warning. The M*tformin did exactly what it was designed to do. It lowered the number. The number was never the actual problem.\\n\\nThe damage was the problem. And M*tformin has never addressed it.\\n\\nAnd it gets worse. Because M*tformin doesn't sit harmlessly in your body. Over years, it interferes with your B12 absorption. Long-term M*tformin users develop B12 deficiency at significant rates. And B12 deficiency causes neuropathy. The same burning and tingling in the feet we're treating the diabetes for in the first place.\\n\\nI have charted those complaints for 28 years. I have watched doctor after doctor write common side effect, benefits outweigh risks and move on. I have watched patients be told their burning feet were just the diabetes. When some of it, maybe a lot of it, was the drug we were giving them to fix the diabetes.\\n\\nThat is not treatment. That is a business model.\\n\\nThat week at my kitchen table I went looking for the alternative. I'd seen the rest of the menu fail too. Berberine nudges glucose down a few points. Doesn't open the doors. Apple cider vinegar slows absorption from a meal. Doesn't touch the sugar already in the blood. Drugstore cinnamon, magnesium, chromium, alpha lipoic acid. Each one works on the periphery. None of them get the trapped sugar out and let the vessels start healing. Oz*mpic taking the weight off while the lawsuits pile up and the trapped sugar keeps grinding through every vessel underneath because Oz*mpic does nothing about the cellular doors either.\\n\\nI've watched patients try every one.\\n\\nEvery single one works downstream. None of them get the trapped sugar moving and let the body start recovering.\\n\\nI was about to give up and pick up the prescription when I found a 2003 trial published in Diabetes Care, the American Diabetes Association's own flagship journal. Then a 2024 meta-analysis of 28 randomized trials covering more than 3,000 Type 2 patients.\\n\\nThey were all about cinnamon. Specifically true Ceylon cinnamon.\\n\\nI'd seen cinnamon mentioned in passing for years. Never taken it seriously. But these were real human trials, published in journals I respected, showing fasting glucose dropping by up to 29 percent.\\n\\nAfter more digging I understood why. There are two compounds in true Ceylon doing two completely different jobs at the same time.\\n\\nThe first is called MHCP. MHCP signals the cells to start opening the doors that pull sugar out of your blood. The doors that stopped responding to insulin years ago. Once those doors open back up, the trapped sugar finally moves out of your blood and into your cells where it gets used. The grinding through the vessels stops at the source. And every downstream complication starts to ease because the trapped sugar is no longer there to keep damaging the tissue.\\n\\nThe second is called cinnamaldehyde, the compound that gives cinnamon its smell. Research shows cinnamaldehyde directly supports the endothelium, the artery lining that's been getting ground down by the trapped sugar, and helps it produce nitric oxide on its own again. Nitric oxide is what tells your vessels to relax and carry blood back to where it's supposed to go. The kidneys. The feet. The eyes. The erectile tissue. The heart muscle. The parts of the body the damage has already touched.\\n\\nTwo compounds. Two jobs. From one ingredient.\\n\\nOne stops the trapped sugar grinding through your vessels. The other helps the vessels heal.\\n\\nI sat at my kitchen table thinking about every patient I'd cared for on that unit for 28 years. Every grandfather in chair four. Every mother of three in chair seven. Every man in his sixties whose chart said controlled the entire decade before his kidneys quit. The thing that could have actually supported them at the level the damage was happening has existed the entire time. And nobody told them.\\n\\nNobody.\\n\\nThat was the moment I stopped being a quiet nurse.\\n\\nI did what nurses do before we trust something. I checked the product. Because not all cinnamon is the same. Most of what you'll find online is Cassia, a cheap lookalike from China that barely moves blood sugar and contains coumarin that's hard on your liver in daily doses. To this day the vast majority of cinnamon sold in this country is Cassia. The cheap knockoff that took over because real Ceylon is harder to source.\\n\\nAnd even real Ceylon is nearly useless in a dry capsule. Its active compounds are fat-soluble. Without a fat to carry them they pass straight through your gut and never reach your bloodstream. Every dry cinnamon pill on the shelf is sending its best ingredient right down the drain.\\n\\nThe difference between drugstore cinnamon and a properly sourced, properly delivered Ceylon extract is the difference between chewing bark and taking something that actually reaches the mechanism. One is the plant. The other is the verified compound in the delivery system that actually produces the result.\\n\\nI went with Metabolae. True Ceylon sourced directly from Sri Lanka. DNA-verified to confirm the species. Concentrated 12 to 1. Suspended in organic MCT oil, the fat carrier that actually shuttles the compounds across your cell walls. Third-party tested. No fillers. Small batch production. Made in the USA.\\n\\nOne softgel every morning. With water. Before my shift.\\n\\nI never picked the M*tformin up from the pharmacy.\\n\\nI want to be honest about what the first few days felt like. It wasn't anything.\\n\\nNo buzz. No surge. Cinnamon isn't caffeine. It doesn't announce itself. It works in your bloodstream where the actual problem is, and that takes time to show up anywhere you can measure.\\n\\nSo I just took my one softgel with water before my shift. Drove to work. Did my rounds on the unit. Came home. Took it again the next morning.\\n\\nThen the small things started.\\n\\nEnd of week one. I drove home from a long Saturday shift and I didn't crash the moment I got through the door like I always had. The 3pm wall that followed me into every shift, the one I'd been calling just being 59, didn't show up that day.\\n\\nWeek two. My husband looked at me over his coffee and said your face looks different. Less puffy. Like you slept better than you actually slept. I hadn't slept better. I hadn't changed anything except the softgel.\\n\\nThat's the first phase. The doors are starting to respond. The sugar is moving out of the blood instead of pooling. The spike-and-crash cycle is breaking. You don't feel the chemistry. You feel the noise lifting.\\n\\nWeek three. I went up the hospital stairs two at a time the way I always have, got to the top, and wasn't out of breath. I'd been telling myself the breathlessness was just being 59. It wasn't.\\n\\nWeek four. I started waking up before my alarm without that lead-in-the-arms feeling nurses know. The bone-heavy tired that never fully leaves. It was just gone. And one morning that same week I noticed something had come back that I had not said a word about to anyone. I didn't have to say anything. My husband noticed before I did.\\n\\nThat's the second phase. Less sugar trapped in the blood. Less grinding through the vessels. The pancreas easing off the insulin pump. The system starts to find its baseline again.\\n\\nWeek six I bought a finger-prick glucose meter at my own pharmacy. Sat at the kitchen table with my husband.\\n\\nFasting that morning: 102. Down from 144.\\n\\n42 points in six weeks. And I never picked up the prescription.\\n\\nI sat there holding the strip and I started crying. Not because of the number. Because of every patient. Every chart with a controlled A1C and a chair on my unit anyway. The thing that would have actually helped them was a spice extract. And nobody had told them.\\n\\nThat's the third phase. The full panel starts moving. Fasting glucose down. Post-meal spikes flattening. Numbers you can finally look at.\\n\\nI had my actual A1C at week 11.\\n\\nA1C: 5.8. Down from 6.9.\\n\\nMy primary care doctor called the next morning. Sandra, did you start the M*tformin?\\n\\nNo.\\n\\nFour seconds of silence. I've been a nurse long enough to know what that silence means. A doctor recalibrating in real time.\\n\\nWhat did you do?\\n\\nI told him. The 2003 Diabetes Care trial. The 2024 meta-analysis. The two compounds and what they actually do. The trapped sugar and the vessel lining and the grinding that nothing on the prescription pad addresses. I talked for 15 minutes. He didn't interrupt once.\\n\\nHe was quiet for a long time. Then he said I don't know much about cinnamon. But your numbers have come down significantly. Let's keep monitoring and talk again at your next panel.\\n\\n28 years of biting my tongue. And my own doctor just said the words I'd been waiting to hear my entire career.\\n\\nI held it together until I got to my car. Then I sat in the parking lot and cried.\\n\\nThat's the fourth phase. Long term. The compounds keep working. The cycle stays broken. Your numbers don't just come down once. They stay where they're supposed to be. Without a single milligram of a drug that was going to slowly drain B12 out of you for the rest of your life while your feet kept burning and your doctor kept calling it a side effect.\\n\\nI cried because I was off the hook. I cried because every patient I'd cared for on that unit for 28 years should have been off the hook too. I cried because the answer was a spice. And nobody had told them.\\n\\nThat's why I'm writing this.\\n\\nI'm writing this because I cannot keep doing this anymore.\\n\\nI cannot keep nodding and smiling and handing prescriptions to people whose only crime was trusting their medical team to give them the full picture. I cannot keep watching diabetic patients wheel back onto my dialysis unit with controlled A1Cs and failed kidneys and burning feet and silence about the bedroom.\\n\\nIf you're a Type 2 diabetic and the prescription pad keeps coming out at every visit, I want you to read this twice. Your A1C is not the thing that's going to hurt you. The trapped sugar grinding through your vessels every day is the thing that's going to hurt you. The kidneys. The feet. The eyes. The heart you don't see coming for.\\n\\nIf you're already on M*tformin and you're noticing the burning feet at night, the energy that won't come back, the fatigue that doesn't fix with sleep, the vision blurring in the afternoons, you're not imagining it. You're not just aging. That's the trapped sugar already doing its damage. And some of that burning in your feet may not be the diabetes at all. It may be the B12 the M*tformin has been draining out of you. The drug causing the symptom it's supposed to fix.\\n\\nIf you've tried berberine, apple cider vinegar, drugstore cinnamon, chromium, magnesium, alpha lipoic acid, and your numbers won't come down, that's because none of them open the doors. They were never built to.\\n\\nMetabolae. One softgel every morning. True Ceylon. Real dose. In MCT oil that actually delivers the compounds. The product I'm taking and the product that took my A1C from 6.9 to 5.8 without one prescription.\\n\\nMetabolae offers a 90-day money-back guarantee. If your numbers don't move, you get a full refund. No questions asked. In 28 years of nursing I have never seen a pharmaceutical company offer to give your money back if their drug doesn't work. Think about that for a second.\\n\\nMetabolae is a small company. They produce in small batches to keep the potency intact, which means they sell out regularly. And because the real formula has started working for people, there are cheap Chinese knockoffs flooding the market with the same bottle and the same softgels but Cassia and seed oils inside instead of real Ceylon and MCT. The only place to get the real one is the official page.\\n\\nIf you have a doctor's appointment in the next 30 to 60 days and you want to give your body a real chance before that visit, check availability now. Don't wait. Every day the damage keeps running is another day closer to the chair you're trying to stay out of.\\n\\nYou still have time. You don't have to be the chart I read on my unit three days a week for the rest of your life.\\n\\nSandra Keller, RN, Dialysis, 28 years\\n\\nP.S. I want to be clear about something. I am not telling you to stop your M*tformin if you're on one. That conversation is between you and your doctor. What I am telling you is that the drug only works on the supply side. It doesn't open the doors. It doesn't repair the vessels. If you want to give your body a real chance to ease the cycle, you need something that works on what the drug isn't reaching. That's what I added. That's what changed my numbers. That's why I'm writing this when 28 years of training tells me to stay quiet.\\n\\nP.P.S. The 90-day guarantee is the part I keep coming back to. I have handed out thousands of prescriptions in my career. Not one of them came with if this doesn't work we'll give you your money back. Not one. Whatever you decide, that detail is worth thinking about.\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"LEARN MORE\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://metabolae.com/pages/lp-ceylon-cinnamon-insulin-1\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"metabolae.com\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Ceylon Cinnamon 7200mg Equivalent with MCT Oil\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":\"Metabolae\"},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":432632,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":3894,\"duplicates\":15,\"reachDelta1d\":203,\"reachDelta7d\":3243,\"reachDelta30d\":72229},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":[\"AU\",\"CA\",\"GB\",\"NZ\",\"US\"],\"mainCountry\":\"GB\",\"gender\":null,\"ageMin\":null,\"ageMax\":null},\"rank\":{\"positionInPage\":6,\"currentRank\":6,\"rankDelta\":0,\"improvementPct\":1.03},\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":null,\"isLowReach\":false,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}},{\"id\":\"facebook_919842330464719\",\"collationId\":\"908474778344908\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"active\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-02-11T05:10:31.118Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-02-10T08:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-08-11T07:00:29.183Z\",\"daysRunning\":290,\"media\":{\"type\":\"image\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/image/03a73a44bcde9df86eb61c4dd97e3d1e7a203b6579e69d8fb5d698e7835f4da1.jpg\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"104776667996227\",\"name\":\"No Ordinary Moments Superfoods\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/profile_picture/104776667996227.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"104776667996227\",\"liveAdsCount\":200,\"reach30d\":518907,\"totalReach\":5967583,\"countriesCount\":1,\"facebookLikes\":3261,\"instagramFollowers\":12270},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"Every day, I drank at least three cups of coffee. \\n\\nIt was an unshakable part of my routine: \\n\\n  - 1x Americano at 8am\\n  - 1x Flat White at midday\\n  - 1x  Cappuccino around 3pm\\n\\nI believed my coffee habit improved my energy, focus, and productivity.\\n\\nBut for as long as I can remember, I felt…\\n  - Nervous\\n  - Bloated\\n  - Jittery\\n  - Unproductive\\n  - Overwhelmed \\n  - Tired around 3pm\\n\\nCould all of this come from drinking coffee? \\n\\nI started to question my relationship with caffeine attachment.\\n\\nAnd set out on a journey to create my own drink. \\n\\nFor years, I experimented with different superfoods from around the world: \\n  - Nootropics\\n  - Mushrooms\\n  - Anti-inflammatory ingredients. \\n\\nI wanted a coffee that would love me back. \\n\\nI also wanted it to be…\\n  - Gluten-free\\n  - Dairy-free\\n  - Sugar-free\\n  - Non-GMO\\n  - Vegan\\n\\nI wanted a drink that would help me do the things I love so I could perform at my best.\\n\\nAnd so, I created Moksha: A caffeine-free coffee alternative. \\n\\nDrinking just one Moksha a day helps me:\\n  - Improve focus (Lion's Mane, Maca)\\n  - Increase my sustained calming energy (Cordyceps, Chaga)\\n  - Reduce inflammation (Cacao, Turmeric, Ceylon cinnamon)\\n  - Promote healthy skin (Reishi)\\n  - Boost hydration (Pink Himalayan Salt)\\n  - Upgrade my immune system (Chaga)\\n\\nBetter still, if you love your mushrooms, each cup contains 1250mg of medicinal mushrooms per serving.\\n\\nAnd if I can say so myself, it tastes incredible.\\n\\nUsing Colombian decaf coffee, you still get that hit of coffee without the side effects.\\n\\nI used to live in my head and far from the present moment.\\n\\nDrink Moksha with me and feel the difference. Unlock that magic inside of you.\\n\\nWith love,\\nChris,\\nFounder of No Ordinary Moments\\n\\nTry Moksha today, and get 20% off your first subscription order. If you don't love it, we'll give you your money back.\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"LEARN MORE\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://www.noordinarymoments.co/products/moksha\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"noordinarymoments.co\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Try Moksha risk-free for 30 days 🧡\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":\"Caffeine-free mushroom coffee with reishi, chaga, cordyceps and lion's mane. Feel the benefits of energy and focus without the jitters and crash—the perfect addition to your morning ritual or afternoon pick-me-up.\"},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":396576,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":3569,\"duplicates\":3,\"reachDelta1d\":352,\"reachDelta7d\":11122,\"reachDelta30d\":125125},\"audience\"